 ## Background
-The concepts of flat and smooth (Gouraud) shading were formalized in early computer graphics APIs, specifically OpenGL, to handle the visual appearance of polygonal meshes [S1, unofficial]. Smooth lighting assumes light comes from a particular direction, requiring a normal vector at each vertex to estimate reflected light intercepted by the observer [S1, unofficial]. Phong shading interpolates normals rather than colors for more accurate specular highlights [G]. Applying these models to Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) leverages numerical gradients for ray-marched geometry [G].
-
+The concepts of flat and smooth (Gouraud) shading originate from early OpenGL specifications, which defined `glShadeModel` to control interpolation behavior [S1, unofficial]. Phong shading, while not explicitly detailed in the provided source, is a standard extension of these principles to include specular reflection. Blinn-Phong was introduced as an optimization to Phong shading. These models are foundational in computer graphics, used in both real-time engines and offline renderers.
